https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow/

Log the model and metrics with MLflow during training, register the model in Unity Catalog using the MLflow API, and deploy the registered model through Databricks Model Serving.

The course wording says “during training” because it is using the classic ML lifecycle example. But MLflow is not only for model training. In GenAI/RAG projects, MLflow is also very useful for tracking pipeline runs, parameters, metrics, artifacts, evaluations, and versions.

So in your GE project, using MLflow for parsing, chunking, embedding, and evaluation is a valid use of MLflow capabilities.


Track pipeline experiments
→ compare parsing/chunking/embedding settings
→ evaluate RAG quality and efficiency

MLflow Tracking/Evaluation
= log what happened and compare quality

MLflow Model Packaging/Registry/Serving
= save a deployable model or workflow and expose it as an endpoint

If you wanted to deploy the whole RAG workflow as one served unit, you could wrap it as an MLflow PyFunc model:

question
  → PyFunc predict()
  → retrieve chunks
  → build prompt
  → call LLM
  → return answer

Then you could:

log PyFunc model with MLflow
→ register it in Unity Catalog
→ serve it with Databricks Model Serving